      "problem": "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}$ as a power of $e$ (the base of the natural logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{1/2}}={e}^{-1/2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})\n$$\n\nThe natural logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-1/2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$",
